%S A402165 0,1,2,1,3,1,2,3,4,1,2,1,3,4,4,1,5,1,2,3,3,1,2,4,4,5,5,1,5,1,2,3,6,3,
%T A402165 2,1,3,4,4,1,4,1,2,5,3,1,5,3,5,6,6,1,6,3,2,3,6,1,2,1,3,4,4,4,7,1,4,3,
%U A402165 3,1,2,1,4,5,5,3,5,1,2,6,5,1,2,6,3
%N A402165 Number of steps to reach 2 starting from n under the map x -> x/lpf(x) + 1, where lpf(x) is the least prime factor of x (A020639).
%C A402165 While iterating x -> x/lpf(x) trivially reaches 1 in A001222(n) steps by stripping prime factors, adding 1 breaks the multiplicative structure and creates a nontrivial arithmetic dynamical system that always converges to 2 (since x/lpf(x) + 1 <= x/2 + 1 < x for every composite x >= 4).
%C A402165 For all n >= 3, a(n) <= floor(log_2(n - 2)) + 1, with equality occurring at n = 2^(k-1) + 2 (A052548) for k >= 1, where a(2^(k-1) + 2) = k.
%C A402165 Compare with A064097, which counts steps to reach 1 by subtracting 1 from primes and dividing composites by prime factors.
%F A402165 a(2) = 0; for n > 2, a(n) = 1 + a(n/A020639(n) + 1).
%F A402165 a(n) = 0 if and only if n = 2.
%F A402165 a(n) = 1 if and only if n is an odd prime (A065091).
%e A402165 For n = 27, the trajectory is 27 -> 27/3 + 1 = 10 -> 10/2 + 1 = 6 -> 6/2 + 1 = 4 -> 4/2 + 1 = 3 -> 3/3 + 1 = 2, taking 5 steps, so a(27) = 5.
%o A402165 (PARI) a(n) = my(c=0); while(n!=2, n = n/factor(n)[1,1] + 1; c++); c;
%o A402165 (Python)
%o A402165 from sympy import primefactors
%o A402165 def a(n):
%o A402165     c = 0
%o A402165     while n != 2:
%o A402165         n = n // primefactors(n)[0] + 1
%o A402165         c += 1
%o A402165     return c
%Y A402165 Cf. A001222, A020639, A052548, A064097, A065091.
%K A402165 nonn,new
%O A402165 2,3
